o to speak from the people most  affected by what you're building  endorsing what you're doing as a  designer  unless you have that then what you're  doing is problematic regardless of bias  regardless of  the date on which was trained anything  like that and i came into to try because  i was interested in the tension between  these two perspectives on this problem  and i i got really interested in  um not so much sort of speaking up as a  humanist to critique which i was doing  externally but rather to try a version  of what i i came to really own as a form  of imminent critique  uh which those of you who are familiar  with critical theory or the marxist  tradition uh you know that imminent  critique is basically a way of trying to  bore into a paradigm from within  uh to uncover within it contradictions  uh that are not resolvable within that  paradigm as a basis for moving from  moving forward and for me that was sort  of a very interesting question of  um what is an imminent critique of ai  what would that mean today uh to to to  become a computer scientist um and to  uh have a profound skepticism in this  paradigm even as i became very  intricately involved in it uh and that  was really the root of of this work for  me so i started learning  uh which i was trying to do and that a  lot of a lot of the um  the technical agenda that they were  pursuing that a safety was pursuing that  they're still pursuing i should say  um is conceptually rooted in a paradigm  that i think was was well expressed by  william mccaskill  he's more often no he's a philosopher  he's more often known as one of the  founders of effective altruism it's a  very prominent kind of ethics movement  on the global scale which i'm happy to  speak a little bit more about during the  q a or the panel if we want um but  mccaskill's view is that basically uh  the way we should go about solving  trolley problems  uh which is to say the kinds of moral  dilemmas that you can formally model uh  and conceivably resolve through some  kind of like revealed or expressed  preference  from whoever you're asking about this  moral dilemma the dilemma of who you  should kill in the case of a trolley  problem  is what he calls metanormativism which  is basically a recourse to more data in  other words well if we knew more about  what is at stake in that dilemma if we  knew more about  the five people that the trolley might  hit or if the one person i might save is  my mother or my aunt or my best friend  or has terminal cancer or something like  that if we dissolve that abstraction by  collecting more information from the  world we can basically use bayesian  inference to solve moral dilemmas that's  basically the idea here that there's  some kind of uncertainty over our  assumptions that we can minimize reduce  the bias and in so doing  um  solve a lot of the ethical and moral  quandaries that that ai is encumbered  with uh when we think about the bias or  the uh the inaccuracy of these systems  being built so  the  the resulting problem stops being a  moral dilemma and turns more into a kind  of practical dilemma of how do we get  how do we leverage enough data how do we  get enough information from the world  from people from institutions  so that uh the assumptions by which our  systems are built are more robustly more  accurately  uh  modeled and incorporated you basically  reduce any kind of like fundamental  tension  uh between moral priorities into a  question of of epistemic inference  and this is a very influential position  i was kind of interested by how  influential it was and how  uninterrogated this was  by many of the people i was actively  collaborating with uh and working with  and you know i'll just i don't want to  dwell on this because i think i'm  speaking to an audience that is maybe  not so sympathetic to this way of  thinking so i'll just sort of briefly  say  um that i was interested in this puzzle  of like well where is the the designer  where is the ai designer putting  themselves in the kind of epistemic  hierarchy that defines whatever the the  system is that they're building if you  deploy  a classifier uh uh for for computer  vision for for you know medical imaging  let's say in a hospital um there's a  vast in largely informal institutionally  backed up division of labor uh comprised  of just you know some forms of expertise  i put on the slide of whose judgment  whose discretion goes into its use its  application what cases is appropriate or  inappropriate for what ratio of false  positives or false negatives are  acceptable it's a it's a vast normative  landscape that you you just can't  resolve by just getting more data  because  that implies some vantage point some  perspective  on the domain that is  not neutral because you're situated in  that domain when you're looking at it  when you're building a system like this  and so i sat with this tension for years  because i knew and still believe  that these assumptions and these ideas  are going to inform what for the next  several decades will define  uh public policy for ai and economic  policy for ai that i think in 20 30  years a lot of the ideas behind ai  safety are going to inform  how the federal reserve works in in 30  or 40 years how we define economic  policy for the remainder of the 21st  century that basically if you think for  the historical parallel here is like the  chicago school of economics in the 50s  or 60s right not really taken seriously  by many other people scene actually is  quite outlandish milton friedman was not  taken seriously in his early career  right up until he was and i was  interested to be on the ground floor of  a place like that i wanted to ask myself  what can i say to these people  about what they're doing how can i how  can i help them and help myself  even make sense of these problems in a  way that uh  actually could be conducive to the ai  paradigm whatever that means  uh how do we move maybe past or  problematize the politics of refusal how  do we enrich it how do we make it  something that can be grocked by  mainstream computer scientists and ai  theorists and so for me  i became much more comfortable with this  language of what what me and some of my  collaborators came to call normative  indeterminacy  uh rather than just uncertainty  uh which i'll just say for the purpose  of this talk had two precepts basically  that social norms  uh are not simply kind of discoverable  through data or encodable  uh in terms of you know your your python  script or your api or whatever it is but  they're also like you're enacting them  by the conditions under which you are  developing these tools  this is an insight that of course goes  back to like second order cybernetics  the idea that the designer of the system  is also somehow implicated in the  feedback the system is taking in or  generating  um and i i basically just you know  refurbished those arguments but tried to  make it copacetic to  uh the style of thinking the patterns  the dispositions of work  uh that were now being pursued you know  in the lab i was a member of and then  secondly um the second kind of point  here is that developing what whatever  safe ai means  uh is is really going to amount among  other things to developing practices  uh that affirm distinct value  commitments that how you comport  yourself as a designer  uh how you professionally determine your  relationship with the system you're  building and the people affected by the  system you're building and how you  justify that relationship  is really at the core of what safety  means  that safety is not just something you  can put in a model or the system you're  building it's something you have to  inhabit  uh in as a designer  and so analytically what that then led  me to was thinking about  going back  thousands of years how have philosophers  tried to make sense of this problem of  indeterminacy so in the paper that this  talk is based on we go into this in more  detail i can say a bit more during the  panel but basically there's this problem  of vagueness that is really central to  the western philosophical tradition  hobbes basically took vagueness as the  jumping off point for the relations of  sovereignty  and vagueness broadly refers to this  problem of the fundamental lack of  clarity and the concepts that we use to  describe ourselves our relationship with  the world how we make sense of what we  perceive and there were basically three  schools of thought that we in this part  in this paper argued were relevant for  ai systems basically that there is  vagueness with respect to perception  there is vagueness with respect to the  language we use to talk about  uh the systems that we are building and  the features that they observe and  there's also possibly a vagueness in our  awareness or lack thereof of the reality  that these systems are meant to refer to  the institutional apparatus the social  domains the applications  that they are meant to work on  and the payoff here was we basically  said all of these positions  these references to indeterminacy  actually manifest materially  at distinct stages of ai development so  basically when you when you hear ai  developers talk about the need to  specify a model the need to define the  features over which you are optimizing  uh  the the concern over who should or  should not have access to the api  what you're actually hearing is in a  practical sense a kind of skidding up  against the boundaries of these  different kinds of vagueness  because at different stages of ai  development we often talk about design  training deployment in the paper we sort  of basically elevate those kind of banal  terms into the kind of philosophical  correlates of  what we mean by ai systems that  interface with the world  what we call in this paper featurization  optimization and then integration  all of which feed into  uh this term we introduced what we call  sociotechnical specification the problem  of defining the relationship  between the system and social reality  and so i'll just briefly for the  remainder of this talk go into a bit  more detail on each of these  these dimensions uh how this  indeterminacy can manifest this is a  very i know smith knows this very well  it's a very famous video um that ai  safety researchers love um of a game uh  computer game in which place here  i think it's called wave runner you  basically have a boat that they've  trained as reinforcement learning that  they used here they they trained an  agent to try to win this race to compete  with the other uh the other boats  and uh they used a proxy uh for  what what this was trying to do  because it's really hard to just sort of  you know train it on win the race so  what they train and always get the most  points they assumed get the most points  meant you're going to win the race as  you can see from the video that's that's  not true uh what the agent has learned  uh is that there's actually this weird  little loopty loop that it could do  uh because there's like two bro boosters  or whatever like that so you can  basically get infinite points  if it learns to not win the race to just  sort of like play its own game so to  speak  what's interesting about this is that  the agent is doing exactly what was told  the agent is not confused the agent is  not stupid the agent is actually smarter  as specified  uh than any human who would play this  game because what it was told to do it's  found the perfect way to do which is get  the most points um and so this problem  of featurization became kind of my way  of referencing this problem of exactly  what are the features of the domain of  the environment  that should matter most  to what is being learned and if you  misspecify that it's going to learn  something that's not so much wrong as  reinterpreting the world in which it's  operating  and so when you hear people talk about  existential risk due to ai when you hear  them talk about the paperclip problem  the problem of defining rewards this is  basically what you're referring to is  that the system is going to end up in a  a recourse where it perverts or contorts  the world  for the sake of what it was told to do i  should add that like marx himself in the  manifesto gave an example of this when  he talks about um basically like he's  drawing on the metaphor of the  sorcerer's apprentice because basically  you're you're teaching the broom so to  speak just like sort of give you  infinite water and marx transposed that  onto the relationship between uh thank  you uh the bourgeoisie and the  proletariat that the proletariat will  naturally kind of rise up and  destroy their masters because they were  told to do the wrong thing  but that's maybe a a different topic um  moving ahead here so basically this is  just to say like accidents will happen  in any domain self-driving cars or  otherwise  when there's some mismatch between the  features that actually matter and those  assumed by the engineer model as the  designer specified them  i'll move ahead here since i'm limited  on time but basically optimization  a little bit more of an abstract problem  than featurization where you're the  problem here is like how is it you're  managing the feedback between the system  and the environment over time so in the  case of like i and actually my read my  subscript research speaks to this  self-driving cars or uber or lyft  ride-sharing fleets  uh basically can perform optimally but  destroy roads at the same time because  of road wear or because they gentrify  cities or because  they induce changes in the preference  distribution of what their consumer  model is of people you can't take the  world for granted when you make these  systems work well  and another form that can take and you  know  online like radicalization with social  media is another politically speaking  um but that the world is material the  world is physical and it will wear down  the better you make these systems  because you're you're doubling down on  features that are convenient for  optimization but otherwise uh you you um  those things need to be sustained in  their own right regardless of whatever  the system has been told to do so this  is a different kind of damage that  happens when there's a when these  sources of feedback are ignored or  mismatched  uh over time sequentially uh generating  unstable conditions for performance  and then finally just to give a brief  example here there's actually most  illustrated best through apis but we  just sort of call it the problem of  integration which is basically this  problem where again with reference to  self-driving cars for example you can  make these systems very safe you can  make them drive accurately you can help  them understand stop signs you can help  them negotiate four-way stops perfectly  but you're still privatizing roads  if you're waymo or your cruise or you're  some other company that's working with  these modules and so this problem of  like well where where exactly is the  publicness of the domain  roads are public they're they're  basically meant to be used however the  public chooses to use them so to  optimize them even in a sustainable way  could still contort them because you're  basically putting yourself in the  position of exercising discretion  over what can or cannot be done with  roads so obviously roads are multimodal  there's this unanswered question of how  pedestrians cyclists other human drivers  other modes of transport are meant to be  sharing the roads with these systems  given that the systems were building avs  automated vehicles might be more optimal  than other forms of transit and that's a  way in which the politics can be  contorted so in the case again of  self-driving cars one of the most common  cases for my phd thesis  um there's this question of like how it  is you're integrating a system in  relationship with infrastructure that  you're not meant to control you're not  in a you're not meant to be in a  position to have that kind of discretion  towards  and again there's an indeterminacy here  about well what is the public interest  once you're building a system that can  squeeze so much more utility out of  infrastructure that was never meant to  be used that way  so just in closing i'll say  what what the result of this of this  framework was that me and my  collaborators were building was the idea  that designers ai designers are really  only in a position  uh to roughly fix the parameters of  system performance when they bear on  these different forms of indeterminacy  precisifying can only get you so far  in ways that that are beyond your  control and really i'll just say you  know as my final word that like this is  really all rooted in this problem that  most ai systems today we still have a  relationship with them that historically  is analogous to like what alchemy was in  you know the 15th 16th 17th centuries  basically where you're in a closet  you're doing your own like fudging with  like whatever this experiment is but  there's no ability to  for the public or any kind of external  actor to weigh in and provide feedback  on whether or not the alchemist is  making accurate decisions about uh the  chemicals you're manipulating in other  words like ai is not scientific  predominantly  and then i think actually to design ai  in the interest of humanity will mean  making it into a more scientific  endeavor where these indeterminacies can  be investigated by  a more diverse group of stakeholders so  i'll end there thank you very much
 

